  "discovered": 1974,
  "discoverers": [
   "Lawrence Berkeley Laboratory, Berkeley",
   "Lawrence Livermore Laboratory, Livermore"
  ],
  "place": "Berkeley, United States",
  "nameOrigin": "After Glenn T. Seaborg, who took part in the discovery of several transuranium elements; it was named in his lifetime.",
  "symbolNote": "A 1994 IUPAC proposal would have given this element the name rutherfordium; seaborgium was adopted in 1997. Temporary name: unnilhexium (Unh).",
  "source": "iupac_discovery",
  "doi": "10.1351/pac199365081757",
  "note": "A Dubna claim from the same year was judged less convincing by the 1993 IUPAC/IUPAP report. Los Alamos wrongly places Seaborg in the Dubna group.",
  "placeLocation": {
   "lat": 37.874,
   "lon": -122.271,
   "source": "natural_earth"
  }
 },
 "prose": {
  "text": "Seaborgium is a synthetic element in group 6, below tungsten, first made at Berkeley in 1974 by firing oxygen-18 ions at californium-249; its atoms of seaborgium-263 lasted about a second. It honours Glenn Seaborg, co-discoverer of plutonium and several other heavy elements, and the name was given while he was still alive, a distinction it shares only with oganesson. Only a few atoms have ever been made, and its longest-lived isotopes survive for roughly a minute or two. Theory assigns it tungsten's outer arrangement, [Rn] 5f¹⁴ 6d⁴ 7s², with a ground level of total angular momentum J = 0, but nothing about its electrons has been measured directly.",
  "sources": [
   "pubchem",
   "iaea_livechart",
   "nist_gsie"
  ]
 }
